{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790286887,"nonce":"ghtINpK8CwYFZVeLK9m5OCuRJp4=","title":"A statement if whose condition is a short-circuit And of two tag tests drops the second test","message":"Seen while lowering Lua arithmetic on two integer-or-nil operands. The frontend emitted, for `t[1] \u003c t[2]` with both reads typed as tagged scalars { tag, int, float }:\n\n    let $a = \u003cread t[1]\u003e; let $b = \u003cread t[2]\u003e;\n    if (($a.0 == 3) \u0026\u0026 ($b.0 == 3)) { } else { zl_lt(box $a, box $b); \u003cpending check, return\u003e }\n    \u003cint compare of $a.1, $b.1\u003e\n\nThe HIR the interpreter tier ran had, in the join block after the second read:\n\n    %v283: i64 = extractvalue %v202, [0]\n    %v284: i64 = eq %v283, %v134\n    brcond %v285, block86, block87      ; %v285 never defined\n    block87: %v340 = extractvalue %v202, [0]; unreachable   ; the raise and the return are gone\n\nand running it failed with \"Execution error: type mismatch: expected bool/int, got Undef\". The same condition built without a short-circuit (the product of the two tags compared with 9) lowers and runs correctly, and BinaryOp::And inside if_value and while conditions elsewhere in the Lua lowering works, so the trigger looks like a short-circuit condition on a statement if whose else branch leaves the function. Reproducer: revert the or_nil_binary tag check in crates/zyntax_lua/src/lower.rs to `present.reduce(|p, q| binary(BinaryOp::And, p, q, bool, span))` and run `local t = { 1, 2, nil, 4 } print(t[1] \u003c t[2])` with ZYNTAX_DUMP_HIR_DIR set.","files":null}]}